" (https://lptk.github.io/files/ctrc-2024-05-09.pdf)\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 30; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://lptk.github.io/files/ctrc-2024-05-09.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1688\nL0@P0: Being Lazy When it Counts\nL1@P0: Practical Constant-Time Memory Management for\nL2@P0: Functional Programming\nL3@P0: Chun Kit Lam[0000−0002−8856−9095] and Lionel Parreaux[0000−0002−8805−0728]\nL4@P0: HKUST {cklamaq,parreaux}@ust.hk\nL5@P0: Abstract. Functional programming (FP) lets users focus on the busi\u0002ness logic of their applications by providing them with high-level and\nL6@P0: composable abstractions. However, both automatic memory management\nL7@P0: schemes traditionally used for FP, namely tracing garbage collection and\nL8@P0: reference counting, may introduce latencies in places that can be hard to\nL9@P0: predict, which limits the applicability of the FP paradigm.\nL10@P0: We reevaluate the use of lazy reference counting in single-threaded func\u0002tional programming with guaranteed constant-time memory management,\nL11@P0: meaning that allocation and deallocation take only a bounded and pre\u0002dictable amount of time. This approach does not leak memory as long as\nL12@P0: we use uniform allocation sizes. Uniform allocation sizes were previously\nL13@P0: considered impractical in the context of imperative programming, but we\nL14@P0: find it to be surprisingly suitable for FP.\nL15@P0: Preliminary benchmark results suggest that our approach is practical, as\nL16@P0: its performance is on par with Koka’s existing state-of-the-art implemen\u0002tation of reference counting for FP, sometimes even outperforming it. We\nL17@P0: also evaluate the effect of different allocation sizes on application perfor\u0002mance and suggest ways of allowing large allocation in non-mission-critical\nL18@P0: parts of the program via Koka’s effect system.\nL19@P0: We believe this potentially opens the door to many new industrial ap\u0002plications of FP, such as its use in real-time embedded software. In fact,\nL20@P0: the development of a high-level domain-specific language for describing\nL21@P0: latency-critical quantum physics experiments was one of the original use\nL22@P0: cases that prompted us to initiate this work.\nL23@P0: 1 Introduction\nL24@P0: Functional programming allows software developers to design applications at a\nL25@P0: very high level of abstraction. It lets them focus on immutability and function\nL26@P0: composition to design programs in a way that can often be described as “correct\nL27@P0: by construction”.\nL28@P0: For example, the functional reactive programming (FRP) pattern [10, 15,\nL29@P0: 22], as found in popular languages like Haskell and Elm, uses immutable data\nL30@P0: structures and higher-order functions to let users declaratively specify reactive\nL31@P0: designs, such as animations, graphical user interfaces, and video games.\nL32@P0: In practice however, a major problem with FRP, as well as with a number of\nL33@P0-1: other functional design patterns, is that it is extremely memory-intensive — on2 Chun Kit Lam and Lionel Parreaux\nL34@P1: each event, the old immutable states of objects that change are discarded and\nL35@P1: reconstructed from scratch, and new closures are allocated to register actions to\nL36@P1: be performed when new events occur. This means that typical FRP programs\nL37@P1: continuously allocate and free lots of memory, which can translate to jerky\nL38@P1: animations and small pauses in graphical user interface rendering.\nL39@P1: Some FRP implementations allow users to avoid needless recomputation by\nL40@P1: telling parent components whether the state has changed [7]. This enables the\nL41@P1: runtime to reuse computation results for unaffected parts of the GUI, which\nL42@P1: reduces memory allocator pressure and makes the program less memory-intensive.\nL43@P1: However, the effectiveness of this kind of optimization depends not only on the\nL44@P1: GUI layout design, but also programmer efforts and discipline. Beginner users\nL45@P1: may not understand the need for such optimization, or may not know for sure if\nL46@P1: the layout is truly unchanged.\nL47@P1: Traditional Memory Management for Functional Programming Func\u0002tional programming languages typically rely on some kind of garbage collection\nL48@P1: for automatic memory management. They often rely on generational, copying\nL49@P1: garbage collectors [9] to efficiently process short-lived objects while performing\nL50@P1: tracing garbage collection for older generations.\nL51@P1: Reference counting, one of the oldest memory management techniques [5]\nL52@P1: [24], was historically considered less practical than garbage collectors in this\nL53@P1: context, because frequent reference count updates can have a large performance\nL54@P1: impact and because reference counting cannot handle cycles [14]. However, there\nL55@P1: has been a recent surge in the popularity of reference counting for general\u0002purpose memory management. Shahriyar et al. [20] analyzed the overhead of\nL56@P1: reference counting compared to tracing garbage collectors and introduced several\nL57@P1: strategies for improving its performance. Ullrich and de Moura [21] showed that\nL58@P1: destructive updates enabled by precise reference counting can provide significant\nL59@P1: performance improvements for functional programming languages. Reinking\nL60@P1: et al. [19] introduced Perceus, an algorithm for precise reference counting with\nL61@P1: destructive updates and specialization, and implemented the algorithm on Koka,\nL62@P1: a functional programming language with a type and effect system. These papers\nL63@P1: showed that functional programming languages using reference counting can have\nL64@P1: good performance too and can compete with state-of-the-art garbage collectors.\nL65@P1: On the other hand, latency is also a key metric for system performance,\nL66@P1: sometimes even more important than throughput, and it has not been directly\nL67@P1: addressed by these recent works. Indeed, eager forms of reference counting can\nL68@P1: often lead to garbage collection pauses, sometimes even longer than those of\nL69@P1: tracing garbage collectors [3]. For example, consider an application that represents\nL70@P1: a news feed as an infinitely-scrollable view on which various widgets can appear.\nL71@P1: Imagine that a particular drawing-board widget interacts with user input by\nL72@P1: letting the user draw shapes in it. Each of these shapes will be represented as\nL73@P1: some vector-graphics object in a collection of shapes that have been drawn so far,\nL74@P1: which could grow very large. Thus, when the user finally loses interest and scrolls\nL75@P1: past the widget, its deallocation in a normal reference counting implementation\nL76@P1-2: will require recursively deallocating an unbounded number of vector-graphicBeing Lazy When it Counts 3\nL77@P2: objects, which is likely to cause a small pause in the scrolling animation, making\nL78@P2: it appear jerky.\nL79@P2: Constant-Time Memory Management for Functional Programming\nL80@P2: In this paper, we revisit the old idea of lazy reference counting, which avoids\nL81@P2: the long delays introduced by cascading deallocations. Our technique works by\nL82@P2: deferring the deallocation of an object’s fields until the memory is needed by\nL83@P2: further allocations, which can progressively reuse memory by traversing the graph\nL84@P2: of objects no longer in use. This effectively gives us constant-time allocation and\nL85@P2: deallocation procedures, which we refer to as constant-time memory management.\nL86@P2: There are naturally a number of limitations and caveats to this approach.\nL87@P2: First, in order to be truly (non-amortized) constant-time without the possibility\nL88@P2: of leaking unbounded amounts of memory, we need all allocations to share the\nL89@P2: same size. While it means that the compiler must split up large objects into\nL90@P2: multiple allocations, we argue that this approach is eminently practical in the\nL91@P2: context of functional programming languages. Indeed, algebraic data type objects\nL92@P2: tend to be on the smaller side, and we show that the performance hit associated\nL93@P2: with splitting larger objects in this context varies from moderate to small or\nL94@P2: insignificant.\nL95@P2: Second, like most reference counting approaches, we do not handle cycles in\nL96@P2: the reference graph. But we argue that in the context of functional programming,\nL97@P2: where most data is immutable and tree-like, cycles can normally be avoided.\nL98@P2: Depending on the compilation strategy and language features, cycles may still be\nL99@P2: introduced in the presence of laziness, recursive closures, or OCaml-style cyclic\nL100@P2: values. With a slight loss of expressiveness, it is possible to design pure functional\nL101@P2: programming languages where cycles cannot be constructed, as exemplified by\nL102@P2: Lean [21]. We argue that the loss of expressiveness is acceptable. On one hand,\nL103@P2: while it is true that recursive closures are traditionally implemented through\nL104@P2: cyclic values, other implementation strategies exist, used for example in languages\nL105@P2: like Rust and Koka. On the other hand, while some languages use laziness to allow\nL106@P2: conveniently constructing cyclic values, it is possible to design more restricted\nL107@P2: languages where laziness is still supported but where cyclic values cannot be\nL108@P2: constructed. Third, our approach is currently only applicable to sequential\nL109@P2: mutators. Due to the lazy nature of our approach, it is impossible in general\nL110@P2: to know the amount of memory actually in use by the program at any given\nL111@P2: time. This makes some approaches to concurrent memory management with\nL112@P2: thread-local free lists impractical.\nL113@P2: In turn, our approach also has large advantages. While the uniform allocation\nL114@P2: size does create internal fragmentation (which could lead to up to 2x memory\nL115@P2: usage overhead in the worst case), it also means that we are effectively free from\nL116@P2: external fragmentation, whose worst-case overhead can become vastly higher\nL117@P2: than 2x, and which often cripples long-running systems in practice, leading to\nL118@P2: degraded performance and even system failure [17].\nL119@P2: We formalized our design, nicknamed CTRC (for constant-time reference\nL120@P2: counting), and implemented it inside the Koka programming language, leveraging\nL121@P2-3: its existing reference counting optimizations as well as its type-and-effect system.4 Chun Kit Lam and Lionel Parreaux\nL122@P3: The allocator adopts the approach of Leijen et al. [13] with page-local free lists\nL123@P3: to improve memory locality.\nL124@P3: Our experimental results show that this approach can achieve throughput\nL125@P3: comparable with Koka using the state-of-the-art mimalloc allocator, while pro\u0002viding constant-time guarantees for both allocation and deallocation, even for\nL126@P3: cases with larger objects that require splitting into several allocations. A major\nL127@P3: advantage of our approach is that it is extremely simple to implement: the basic\nL128@P3: runtime is about 160 lines of C code (the entirety of which fits into four pages of\nL129@P3: Appendix B), and does not make use of any existing system allocator beyond\nL130@P3: the operating system’s memory page allocation routines.\nL131@P3: This could enable the implementation of efficient automatic memory recla\u0002mation for resource-constrained embedded systems, which could have future\nL132@P3: applications in the domain of hard real-time systems.\nL133@P3: Contributions\nL134@P3: – Although similar solutions were proposed in the past in slightly different\nL135@P3: contexts back in 1963 [24], we present a refreshed and practical solution\nL136@P3: to constant-time memory management for functional programming, called\nL137@P3: CTRC (Section 2). CTRC was implemented by adapting the existing Koka\nL138@P3: runtime system and compiler. Our implementation is extremely simple, which\nL139@P3: we consider to be a major selling point.\nL140@P3: – We formalize this new approach and show that it is sound and prevents\nL141@P3: memory leaks, in the sense that it does not increase the peak memory usage\nL142@P3: of programs (Section 2.4 and Appendix A). We also show that CTRC can be\nL143@P3: applied only locally, to those parts of a program that are latency-sensitive.\nL144@P3: – We experimentally justify the practicality of CTRC, showing that on our\nL145@P3: benchmark programs, its space and time overheads are small when compared\nL146@P3: to Perceus, a state-of-the-art reference-counting implementation (Section 3).\nL147@P3: We also discuss the source of the overhead, and suggest approaches for\nL148@P3: programmers to reduce the overhead.\nL149@P3: 2 Presentation of Constant-Time Reference Counting\nL150@P3: In this section, we present our basic idea of constant-time reference counting for\nL151@P3: Functional Programming (CTRC).\nL152@P3: 2.1 Eager and Lazy Reference Counting\nL153@P3: Reference counting is typically implemented by attaching an integer reference\nL154@P3: count to each object, indicating the number of pointers pointing to the current\nL155@P3: object. When pointers or variables are modified, the reference count of the\nL156@P3: referred object is updated. As the reference count of an object becomes zero, it\nL157@P3: is deleted and the reference counts of its fields are decremented. For example,\nL158@P3-4: when deallocating a linked list, the reference count of the first node becomesBeing Lazy When it Counts 5\nL159@P4: zero, which recursively decrements the subsequent nodes and deletes them. We\nL160@P4: shall refer to this type of reference counting as eager reference counting.\nL161@P4: Eager reference counting allows immediate garbage collection with low pause\nL162@P4: times compared to tracing garbage collectors, as the tracing part is done incremen\u0002tally by tracking reference counts rather than a separate tracing phase. However,\nL163@P4: reference counting can still have unbounded pause time as a deallocation event\nL164@P4: can trigger a chain of deallocation.\nL165@P4: By contrast, CTRC performs reference counting lazily. Instead of updating\nL166@P4: reference counts for the fields of deallocated objects immediately, the updates are\nL167@P4: postponed by storing the deallocated objects in a free list. When there is a new\nL168@P4: allocation request, the fields of the object are freed as the object allocation is\nL169@P4: being reused. This breaks the chain of deallocation mentioned previously, making\nL170@P4: the allocation and deallocation of objects constant-time.\nL171@P4: The use of constant-time reference counting mandates using only a single\nL172@P4: allocation size, as using multiple allocation sizes may result in memory leaks,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://raphaelposs.com/academia/publications/pub/poss.12.interact.pdf)\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://raphaelposs.com/academia/publications/pub/poss.12.interact.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 600\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1908.05647] Counting Immutable Beans: Reference Counting Optimized for Purely Functional Programming (https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.05647)\nciteturn5view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.05647\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\n--------------------------------------